Cascadia: a draw that textures from the framebuffer it renders into makes
PPSSPP copy that framebuffer first (BindFramebufferAsColorTexture:
BlitFramebuffer into a temporary FBO) -- God of War's bloom, six times a
frame into its 864 x 272 target, each copy a render of its own on the iPad 2
(~0.45 ms of the GPU each).  Mesa's SGX driver (a tile-based GPU: renderer
"PowerVR SGX543MP2", "Mesa" in the version) ends the render a draw that
samples its own target would join, so the draw reads all that was drawn
before it, and the GPU stores a tile's pixels only when the tile is done: a
draw that reads none of the pixels it draws needs no copy.  There, in
through mode, the pixels drawn (those whose centres the decoded vertices'
box covers) and the texels read (the two around each texture coordinate,
kept in the texture's rectangle at its offset when PPSSPP's shader clamps
them there) are compared; apart, the target is bound as the texture itself.
DrawEngineGLES::Flush hands the decoded vertices over while it applies the
texture, on either transform path (PPSSPP's own texture coordinate bounds
are not kept by every vertex decoder: ARM32's JIT skips float ones).  Every
bloom draw of God of War qualifies: each draws x 0..256 and reads its
texture at offset 256, clamped.
PPSSPP_SELF_TEXTURE_COPY=1 / 0: a copy always / none where the draw reads
none of what it draws, whatever the driver.


